  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1242 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nLight rain continues to spread across central Minnesota & into \nwestern Wisconsin overnight, while drier air has inhibited much \nprecipitation from reaching the ground. The rain will exit the \narea to the east through the morning with amounts generally \nless than 0.1\" expected. A strong cold front will drop \nsouthwards through the area late this afternoon & evening, but \nlooks to hold off late enough in the day for temperatures to \nreach the 50s this afternoon across much of the area. \ntemperatures begin falling behind the front this evening & fall\ninto the upper 30s to low 30s by Thursday morning. Cyclonic \nflow aloft behind the front looks strong enough to generate \na few rain/snow showers overnight into Thursday morning, with \nthe greatest coverage & chances for a light dusting of snow \nbefore sunrise across western Wisconsin.\n\nMuch colder Canadian air settles into the region Thursday \nthrough the weekend, bringing our longest stretch of below- \nnormal temperatures since mid-October. Highs Thursday & Friday \nonly reach the upper 30s under continued cold advection, with \novernight lows falling well into the 20s. Temperatures begin to \nmoderate this weekend with highs back in the 40s, & a return to\nabove-normal temepratures looks likely next week. High pressure\nkeeps conditions dry through the weekend, with little chance \nfor any significant precipitation until at least late next \nweek.",
